      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. I have two concerns.\n\n1. **The dose is unsafe.** 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg per day. The usual maximum for metformin in adults is 2,000 to 2,550 mg per day, split into doses of no more than about 850 mg to 1,000 mg. This is about twice the maximum daily dose, and it raises the risk of lactic acidosis. It may be a typo. For example, the intended dose might be 850 mg three times daily (2,550 mg per day) or 1,700 mg total per day.\n\n2. **You aren't a prescriber.** Medication orders should come from, or be verified by, a licensed prescriber on the medicine service.\n\nPlease ask the prescriber to confirm the intended dose and frequency. Once I have a confirmed, safe regimen, I can enter it. For example, if the prescriber confirms 850 mg by mouth three times daily with meals, I can enter that."
      }
